package constant

// Package constant holds the exact values of integer and float constant
// expressions. It is a port of the Go compiler's package constant.
//
// Tack computes a constant expression, such as 1 + 2 * 3 written as
// literals and operators, exactly, before giving it a type. Every literal
// and every intermediate result must lie between -2^63 and 2^64-1: the
// smallest int64 through the largest uint64, which covers every value of
// every integer type. A result outside that range is an error rather than
// a wider number.
//
// An Int is a sign and a 64-bit magnitude. Each operation returns its
// result and whether it is in range. Division truncates toward zero and a
// remainder takes the sign of the dividend, as in Go and C. The bitwise
// operators work on a value's infinitely wide two's complement form,
// which 65 bits hold for every value in range (see bits.tck); a left
// shift is exact and a right shift rounds toward negative infinity. fits
// reports whether a value belongs to a particular integer type. Go's
// Neg, Not, And, Or and Xor are negate, complement, bit_and, bit_or and
// bit_xor here, since a field is named neg and the others read as
// keywords elsewhere.
//
// A float constant is an exact rational, Go's Float, here num / den
// over std/big's integers (rat.tck), whose numerator and denominator in
// lowest terms may each take at most max_bits bits. Go's big.Rat keeps
// them in lowest terms always; here they are reduced only when either
// reaches 2^max_bits, which gives the same values and the same answers
// about them for less work. float_literal_ok decides whether a literal
// can be held, which the parser asks, and parse_float gives its value,
// which the type checker computes with. float64_bits and float32_bits
// round a value to the nearest float, ties to even, as Go's big.Rat
// does, giving the float's bits, since the port has no float arithmetic
// of its own to hold one in, and whether it is finite.
//
// It is a leaf package: the parser decodes literals into it, and the
// later stages will compute with it.
